  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B046",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for the invention and experimental development of optical lattice clocks"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B010",
    "B005"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "I change my preferred configuration from B010 to B046. The discussion has clarified the distinction between a profound principle that remains under demanding development and an achievement whose defining physical performance is already repeatedly realized. Optical lattice clocks unite a sharply identifiable quantum-control idea with experimentally mature, independently reproduced 10^-18 precision. They make exceptionally small frequency shifts physically accessible in a controlled ensemble system; this is a new regime of measurement, not simply an incremental improvement. Katori and Ye are a compact concept-to-realization attribution, provided the citation remains limited to lattice clocks.",
    "case_against": "The historical boundary remains important: lattice clocks rest on a wider development of optical clocks, frequency metrology, atomic control, and precision spectroscopy. We should not use the citation to absorb that broader history, and archival review must test whether the two proposed names fully represent the specific lattice-clock advance. B010 remains more conceptually far-reaching, but its proposed grouping joins several foundational strands and sustained fully fault-tolerant operation is not yet established.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll argues that lattice clocks have demonstrated independently reproduced 10^-18 performance with controlled systematics, and that the prize should concern the controlled lattice-clock method rather than a future redefinition of the second.",
        "response": "I agree. This is the decisive comparative consideration for me: a prize need not await an administrative metrological milestone, but it should recognize a physical advance whose core claim has survived stringent independent operation. The citation should remain narrower than optical frequency metrology as a whole."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "lindroth-eva",
        "point": "Lindroth regards B010 as profound but experimentally incomplete, whereas B046 connects a delimited principle to a mature operating platform.",
        "response": "I now share that ordering. My original support for B010 was based on the fundamental insight that encoded dynamics can control local noise. Yet its final physical realization and exact three-person credit remain less bounded than I initially weighted. B046 offers the cleaner completed discovery without making downstream applications its main justification."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "eriksson-olle",
        "point": "Eriksson argues that DMFT has a long record of quantitative explanatory power but requires a careful separation of the original framework from its extensive ecosystem of solvers and extensions.",
        "response": "That is a useful standard for all technically generative achievements. It reinforces my caution about both B004 and B010: broad later ecosystems can obscure the prize-defining unit. For B046, the same discipline supports a tightly limited citation to the lattice-clock concept and experimental development, with the residual attribution question explicitly retained for review."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether archival review supports credit limited to Katori and Ye for the specifically defined lattice-clock achievement.",
      "How to draw a citation boundary that distinguishes optical lattice clocks from the wider development of optical frequency metrology.",
      "Whether future sustained fault-tolerant operation would make B010 the stronger candidate in a later year."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "No direct connection to Katori or Ye is identified in my supplied profile. As director of a national quantum-technology programme, I have a professional interest in the broad quantum-information field and have therefore reassessed B010 cautiously; the supplied evidence identifies no specific conflict requiring recusal."
  }
}
